<h2 id="13-deployment-railway">13. Deployment (Railway)</h2>
<h3 id="131-hosting-overview">13.1 Hosting Overview</h3>
<p>The application is deployed to <strong>Railway</strong>, a managed
platform-as-a-service that handles server infrastructure, OS updates,
database backups, and SSL certificates automatically. You push code and
it runs -- no server management required.</p>
<p><strong>Estimated monthly cost: ~$5-10/month</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Service</th>
<th>Cost</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Railway Hobby Plan</td>
<td>$5/month base</td>
<td>Includes $5 of usage credits</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Rails App (web)</td>
<td>Usage-based</td>
<td>CPU + memory consumed per second</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>PostgreSQL</td>
<td>Usage-based</td>
<td>Runs as a Railway service, same billing model</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>SSL</td>
<td>$0</td>
<td>Auto-provisioned by Railway</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Builds</td>
<td>$0</td>
<td>Included in plan</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>For a low-traffic church site, the $5 in included credits should
cover most or all usage. If usage exceeds the included credits, you pay
only for the overage.</p>
<h3 id="132-prerequisites">13.2 Prerequisites</h3>
<ol type="1">
<li>A GitHub account (Railway connects directly to your repo)</li>
<li>The Railway CLI (optional but useful):
<code>npm install -g @railway/cli</code></li>
<li>Your code pushed to a GitHub repository</li>
</ol>
<h3 id="133-one-time-setup">13.3 One-Time Setup</h3>
<h4 id="step-1-create-a-railway-account-and-project">Step 1: Create a
Railway Account and Project</h4>
<ol type="1">
<li>Sign up at <a href="https://railway.com/">railway.com</a> using your
GitHub account</li>
<li>Select the <strong>Hobby</strong> plan ($5/month)</li>
<li>Click <strong>New Project</strong> &gt; <strong>Deploy from GitHub
repo</strong></li>
<li>Select your <code>wbr-pres-cms</code> repository</li>
<li>Railway will auto-detect the Dockerfile and begin building</li>
</ol>
<h4 id="step-2-add-a-postgresql-database">Step 2: Add a PostgreSQL
Database</h4>
<ol type="1">
<li>In your Railway project dashboard, click <strong>New</strong> &gt;
<strong>Database</strong> &gt; <strong>PostgreSQL</strong></li>
<li>Railway provisions the database service</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Important:</strong> Railway does NOT automatically inject a
resolved <code>DATABASE_URL</code> into your web service. The Postgres
service's <code>DATABASE_URL</code> variable is itself a template string
(e.g., <code>postgresql://${{PGUSER}}:...</code>) that does not resolve
when cross-referenced between services. You must manually construct the
<code>DATABASE_URL</code> in your web service using the actual values
from the Postgres service's Variables tab.</p>
<h4 id="step-3-set-environment-variables">Step 3: Set Environment
Variables</h4>
<p>In the Railway dashboard, go to your web service &gt;
<strong>Variables</strong> and add:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Variable</th>
<th>Value</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>DATABASE_URL</code></td>
<td>See construction guide below</td>
<td>Must be manually set — see note</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>RAILS_MASTER_KEY</code></td>
<td>Contents of <code>config/master.key</code></td>
<td>Copy the key value, not the file path</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>RAILS_ENV</code></td>
<td><code>production</code></td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>RAILS_SERVE_STATIC_FILES</code></td>
<td><code>true</code></td>
<td>Railway doesn't use a separate web server</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA</code></td>
<td><code>true</code></td>
<td>Runs background jobs inside the web process</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Constructing <code>DATABASE_URL</code> manually:</strong></p>
<p>Railway's cross-service <code>${{Postgres.DATABASE_URL}}</code>
reference does not work because that variable is itself a template.
Instead, go to the Postgres service → Variables tab and find the literal
values for <code>POSTGRES_USER</code>, <code>POSTGRES_PASSWORD</code>,
<code>POSTGRES_DB</code>, and the TCP proxy domain/port
(<code>RAILWAY_TCP_PROXY_DOMAIN</code>,
<code>RAILWAY_TCP_PROXY_PORT</code>). Then set <code>DATABASE_URL</code>
in the web service to:</p>
<pre><code>postgresql://postgres:&lt;POSTGRES_PASSWORD&gt;@&lt;RAILWAY_TCP_PROXY_DOMAIN&gt;:&lt;RAILWAY_TCP_PROXY_PORT&gt;/railway</code></pre>
<p>Alternatively, enable <strong>Private Networking</strong> in Railway
project settings and use the <code>.railway.internal</code>
hostname.</p>
<p>Cloudinary credentials are stored in Rails encrypted credentials
(<code>bin/rails credentials:edit</code>), which are decrypted at
runtime using <code>RAILS_MASTER_KEY</code>. No need to set them as
separate environment variables.</p>
<h4 id="step-4-configure-configdatabaseyml-for-production">Step 4:
Configure <code>config/database.yml</code> for Production</h4>
<p>Rails automatically uses <code>DATABASE_URL</code> when it's present
in the environment, which overrides the <code>database.yml</code>
settings. However, for the auxiliary databases (cache, queue, cable)
that use Solid adapters, update the production config to use the same
database:</p>
<pre class="yaml"><code>production:
  primary:
    &lt;&lt;: *default
    url: &lt;%= ENV[&quot;DATABASE_URL&quot;] %&gt;
  cache:
    &lt;&lt;: *default
    url: &lt;%= ENV[&quot;DATABASE_URL&quot;] %&gt;
    migrations_paths: db/cache_migrate
  queue:
    &lt;&lt;: *default
    url: &lt;%= ENV[&quot;DATABASE_URL&quot;] %&gt;
    migrations_paths: db/queue_migrate
  cable:
    &lt;&lt;: *default
    url: &lt;%= ENV[&quot;DATABASE_URL&quot;] %&gt;
    migrations_paths: db/cable_migrate</code></pre>
<p>This consolidates all databases into the single PostgreSQL instance
provided by Railway, keeping costs down.</p>
<h4 id="step-5-enable-ssl-in-production">Step 5: Enable SSL in
Production</h4>
<p>In <code>config/environments/production.rb</code>, uncomment or
add:</p>
<pre class="ruby"><code>config.assume_ssl = true
config.force_ssl = true</code></pre>
<h4 id="step-6-configure-custom-domain-optional">Step 6: Configure
Custom Domain (Optional)</h4>
<ol type="1">
<li>In the Railway dashboard, go to your web service &gt;
<strong>Settings</strong> &gt; <strong>Networking</strong></li>
<li>Add your custom domain (e.g., <code>cms.yourchurch.org</code>)</li>
<li>Railway provides the DNS records to add at your domain
registrar</li>
<li>SSL is provisioned automatically once DNS propagates</li>
</ol>
<p>Without a custom domain, Railway provides a free
<code>*.up.railway.app</code> URL.</p>
<h3 id="134-how-railway-builds-the-app">13.4 How Railway Builds the
App</h3>
<p>Railway detects the <code>Dockerfile</code> in the project root and
uses it to build the application. The existing multi-stage Dockerfile
already handles:</p>
<ul>
<li>Installing Ruby and dependencies</li>
<li>Running <code>bundle install</code></li>
<li>Precompiling assets (Tailwind CSS + import maps)</li>
<li>Setting up Thruster for HTTP compression</li>
</ul>
<p>Railway runs the container using the <code>CMD</code> from the
Dockerfile (<code>./bin/thrust ./bin/rails server</code>). The
<code>bin/docker-entrypoint</code> script automatically runs
<code>db:prepare</code> on startup, which creates and migrates the
database.</p>
<h3 id="135-first-deployment-walkthrough">13.5 First Deployment
Walkthrough</h3>
<pre class="bash"><code># 1. Push your code to GitHub
git push origin main

# 2. Railway auto-deploys from GitHub (check dashboard for build status)

# 3. Once deployed, run database seeds via Railway CLI
railway run bin/rails db:seed

# 4. Create admin user via Railway CLI
railway run bin/rails console
&gt; User.create!(email_address: &quot;admin@yourchurch.org&quot;, password: &quot;secure_password&quot;, password_confirmation: &quot;secure_password&quot;)

# 5. Verify the site is live (use your Railway URL or custom domain)
curl https://wbr-pres-cms.up.railway.app/up</code></pre>
<p>The Railway CLI is the only way to run one-off commands against the
production environment. There is no Shell tab in the Railway dashboard
UI.</p>
<h3 id="136-ongoing-deployments">13.6 Ongoing Deployments</h3>
<p>Railway auto-deploys every time you push to the <code>main</code>
branch on GitHub:</p>
<pre class="bash"><code># Make changes, commit, and push -- Railway deploys automatically
git add .
git commit -m &quot;Update event form&quot;
git push origin main</code></pre>
<p>You can monitor deployments in the Railway dashboard, which shows
build logs, deploy status, and resource usage.</p>
<h3 id="137-useful-railway-cli-commands">13.7 Useful Railway CLI
Commands</h3>
<pre class="bash"><code>railway login                     # Authenticate with Railway
railway link                      # Link local project to Railway project
railway run bin/rails console     # Rails console on production
railway run bin/rails db:migrate  # Run migrations
railway run bin/rails db:seed     # Run seeds
railway logs                      # Tail production logs
railway status                    # Check service status</code></pre>
